#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:1
msgid "Promises"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:3
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"*Implemented.* The answer to the one thing {doc}`pages` cannot handle: a "
"value the page needs but does not carry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:6
msgid "The idea: a named placeholder"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:7
msgid ""
"A value a page cannot resolve on its own becomes a **promise** — a named "
"placeholder — and the run continues. Nothing waits, nothing is re-read, "
"and the page stays the unit of work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:19
msgid ""
"Neither page knows about the other. Page 3 does not know anyone wants "
"that name; page 412 does not know where it will come from. They agree "
"only on an **id**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:22
msgid "The three stages: collection, flattening, resolution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:23
msgid ""
"**1. Collection.** As pages are deserialized, every pipe deposits `(id, "
"value)` pairs into a **multimap**. A multimap, not a map, because several"
" pages legitimately contribute to the same id: a fund name printed at the"
" head of every page of its section, a total repeated at the foot of each "
"table. The first contribution is not privileged and the last does not "
"overwrite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:28
msgid ""
"**2. Flattening.** When the document is finished, references between "
"promises are followed and replaced, and every id keeps its own *sequence*"
" of contributions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:31
msgid ""
"**3. Resolution.** Entities with pending fields are resolved against the "
"flattened map."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:33
msgid "Three properties of promises that are not obvious"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:34
msgid ""
"These are the parts that bite people writing a pipe, and each was decided"
" against a plausible alternative."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:37
msgid "A container is not a contribution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:44
msgid ""
"Flattening synthesises no lists, so the two stay distinguishable, and a "
"pipe wanting to deposit two values must return two dicts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:47
msgid ""
"This distinction is the reason the module exists in its current form. An "
"earlier design conflated them, and the conflation is **unrecoverable**: "
"once you cannot tell \"two values\" from \"one list\", no later stage can"
" work it out. It matters exactly when the value being promised *is "
"itself* a list — rare, and silently wrong when it happens, which is the "
"worst combination."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:52
msgid ""
"Changing it cost authors nothing, which was verified before the change: a"
" pipe returning a list of dicts was already flattened, so the capability "
"existed and only the ambiguity was removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:55
msgid "A null is a non-contribution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:57
msgid ""
"Discarded during flattening as if never deposited. An id whose "
"contributions were **all** null vanishes exactly like an id that never "
"had any — there is no third state of \"present but empty\" for a later "
"stage to interpret differently from the first two."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:61
msgid "A pending reference is not an error where it is noticed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:63
msgid ""
"A promise pointing at an unknown id **survives flattening**. The policy "
"is decided later, at resolution: a non-strict promise makes its entity "
"disappear, a strict one raises an error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:66
msgid ""
"The reason is a diagnostic one. If flattening failed on an unresolved "
"reference, \"circular\" would become the message people see for every "
"missing value, and would stop meaning anything. Deferring it keeps a "
"**circular error always a real cycle**, never a missing-value report "
"wearing the wrong name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:70
msgid "Resolution has three outcomes, each with a name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:72
msgid "Not an `Option<Vec<_>>` for the caller to interpret:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md
msgid "Outcome"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md
msgid "Means"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md
msgid "resolved"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md
msgid "the entity's pending fields are filled in place"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md
msgid "dropped"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md
msgid "a non-strict promise had nothing to resolve to; the entity disappears"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md
msgid "**multiplied**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md
msgid "a field marked *multiple* expands its entity into one copy per value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:80
msgid ""
"Multiplication is a cartesian product when more than one field is "
"multiple. Ordinary fields resolve **first** and multiple fields expand "
"**second**, so the copies are made from an already-resolved entity rather"
" than resolving the same fields once per copy — the same answer, computed"
" once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:84
msgid "Promise maps are ordered, never hashed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/reference/design/promises.md:85
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Insertion or key order, never hash order. For equal content the cycle a "
"failure reports is always **the same** cycle, so an error message can be "
"asserted in a test. See {doc}`determinism`."
msgstr ""

